---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/neurobiology-basics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Biology lesson. Three foundational lessons are prerequisites:
    <strong>cell structure</strong> (the neuron is a specialised cell), <strong>movement
    into and out of cells</strong> (membrane potentials and diffusion are the signal itself)
    and <strong>human body systems</strong> (nerves and hormones are the body's two
    coordinators). The energy bill for all this thinking is paid by the
    <strong>photosynthesis and respiration</strong> lesson.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>label a neuron and trace a reflex arc from receptor to effector;</li>
    <li>explain what happens at a synapse, and why every mind-affecting drug acts there;</li>
    <li>describe the eye as the model sense organ — rods, cones and transduction;</li>
    <li>compare nervous with hormonal coordination using speed, spread and duration;</li>
    <li>apply negative feedback to blood glucose and temperature, and read plant tropisms as the plant's version of coordination.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Neurobiology studies the <strong>cells and circuits behind behaviour</strong> — the biological
    layer between chemistry and psychology. Its ground truth: behaviour is electrochemical.
    Signals travel as spikes along neurons, chemicals carry them across synapses, and circuits of
    repeated use get stronger. But the nervous system is only half of the body's coordination
    story: hormones broadcast slower chemical messages through the blood, and together the two
    systems hold the body's internal world — temperature, glucose, water — steady while the
    external one swings. This lesson covers the whole coordination toolkit: neurons and reflex
    arcs, synapses, the eye as the model sense organ, hormonal control, homeostasis, and the
    plant version of steering. Brain, meet hardware.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four principles that organise nervous systems" widget="equationStep" config={neurobioCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order — they are the
    spine of the whole lesson. Step 1 is speed (reflexes before brains), step 2 the chemical
    handover at the synapse, step 3 the rewiring that learning is made of, step 4 the bill:
    two per cent of body mass burning about twenty per cent of the energy budget. Watch how
    each step answers the leftover question of the one before it.
  </p>

  <h2>Neurons and the reflex arc</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>neuron</strong> is a cell built for speed: a cell body containing the nucleus, fine
    <strong>dendrites</strong> that receive signals, and one long <strong>axon</strong> that sends
    them. The signal is an <strong>action potential</strong> — a self-propagating pulse of
    voltage that reverses the axon's membrane potential from about −70 mV to +30 mV and back
    within roughly a millisecond, then races down the axon as neighbouring sections fire in
    turn. Conduction speeds range from about 0.5 m/s in thin unmyelinated pain fibres to over
    100 m/s (360 km/h) in thick, myelin-insulated motor axons — the fatty myelin sheath forces
    the signal to jump between gaps, multiplying speed twenty-fold. The pulse is all-or-nothing:
    a weak stimulus fires fewer spikes, not weaker ones. Intensity is encoded in <em>frequency</em>,
    like a digital signal.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pin the resting potential on the millivolt scale" widget="place" config={nbPotentialPlace} />
  <p>
    The wiring plan that makes animals fast is the <strong>reflex arc</strong>. Touch something
    hot and the sequence runs: <strong>receptor</strong> (skin) → <strong>sensory neuron</strong>
    → <strong>relay neuron</strong> in the spinal cord → <strong>motor neuron</strong> →
    <strong>effector</strong> (muscle pulls the hand back). The brain is informed afterwards —
    by roughly 0.2–0.5 seconds — but the withdrawal has already happened. Skipping deliberation
    buys speed, and speed matters more than finesse when the alternative is a burned hand. The
    knee-jerk (patellar) reflex is even more direct, with only two neurons and no relay: the
    doctor's hammer taps the tendon, the stretch receptor fires, and the quadriceps contracts.
    Reflexes are involuntary, automatic, and located in the spinal cord — three properties every
    exam answer combines: they are <em>fast</em> (few synapses), <em>protective</em> (they prevent
    injury), and <em>unlearned</em> (wired from birth).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> neurons carry all-or-nothing voltage pulses, and the reflex arc
    wires receptor to effector through the spinal cord so fast the brain is informed only
    afterwards. The next question is what happens when the signal reaches the end of the
    line — and the answer is chemistry.
  </p>

  <h2>The synapse: chemistry between cells</h2>
  <p>
    Neurons don't touch. At a <strong>synapse</strong> there is a gap of roughly 20 nanometres,
    and the electrical spike cannot jump it. Instead the arriving spike triggers vesicles of
    <strong>neurotransmitter</strong> to fuse with the membrane and spill their cargo into the
    gap; molecules diffuse across in about half a millisecond and bind receptors on the next
    cell, opening ion channels and (usually) provoking a new spike. The chemicals are
    polarity-specific: <strong>glutamate</strong> excites, <strong>GABA</strong> inhibits,
    <strong>acetylcholine</strong> fires muscles. Synapses act as one-way valves (vesicles only
    exist on the sending side), as decision points (one neuron integrates many inputs, some
    excitatory, some inhibitory — firing only if excitation wins), and as amplifiers (a single
    vesicle holds thousands of transmitter molecules). They are also the body's great drug
    interface: <strong>every drug that affects the mind acts here</strong> — caffeine blocks
    adenosine's sleep signal, nicotine mimics acetylcholine, Prozac prolongs serotonin's stay in
    the gap, botulinum toxin blocks vesicle fusion entirely. The 20-nanometre gap is where mind
    meets molecule.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each chemical with its synaptic move" widget="match" config={nbDrugMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "electrical signals flow through the brain like wires." No
    current crosses between neurons at all — each synapse is a chemical relay. That is why
    synaptic transmission takes about 0.5 ms per junction (slower than electricity in copper by
    a factor of millions per metre) and why reaction times are dominated by synapse count: the
    fastest human reactions, around 150 ms, are circuits with as few relays as the body allows.</p>

  <h2>Sense organs: the eye as the model</h2>
  <p>
    A sense organ is receptors plus supporting machinery, and the <strong>eye</strong> shows the
    pattern best. Light enters through the transparent <strong>cornea</strong>, which does most
    of the focusing (about two-thirds of it); the <strong>lens</strong> fine-tunes the focus —
    changing shape (accommodation) to throw a sharp image on the <strong>retina</strong>. The
    iris adjusts the pupil's diameter, trading depth of field against light admission. The
    retina's photoreceptors are of two kinds: <strong>rods</strong> (about 120 million, extremely
    sensitive, no colour — night and peripheral vision) and <strong>cones</strong> (about 6
    million, three types for red, green and blue, packed into the fovea where you read this
    text). Their signals leave through the optic nerve — and the brain does the heavy lifting,
    inverting the image, filling the blind spot, and building the visual world. Other sense
    organs follow the same recipe: the ear's cochlea turns pressure waves into nerve spikes via
    hair cells tuned to frequency, the tongue's taste buds and the nose's olfactory receptors
    are chemical sensors. A "sense" is always transduction: one form of energy converted into
    spikes the brain can count.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file the eye's parts by whose job they are" widget="sort-buckets" config={nbEyeBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "rods see colour and cones work at night." It is the
  reverse, and the anatomy explains why: cones need brighter light but come in three
  wavelength types for red, green and blue, so they own colour and daytime detail at the
  fovea; rods are so sensitive that bright light saturates them, so they own dim, colourless
  night and peripheral vision. That is also why a faint star is easier to see slightly
  off-centre — you are putting its image on rods instead.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the retina answer" widget="spot-error" config={nbRodSpot} />

  <h2>Hormones: the broadcast network</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>endocrine system</strong> coordinates by chemistry in the bloodstream.
    <strong>Hormones</strong> are chemicals secreted by glands (pituitary, thyroid, pancreas,
    adrenal, gonads) that affect target cells carrying the matching receptor — every other cell
    ignores them. Where nerves are fast, specific and short-lived (milliseconds), hormones are
    slower (seconds to days), widespread and lasting. Insulin and glucagon manage blood glucose;
    adrenaline prepares the whole body for emergency; thyroxine sets metabolic rate; ADH (from
    the pituitary) governs kidney water reabsorption; oestrogen and testosterone drive
    reproduction. The two systems constantly interact: the brain commands glands via the
    pituitary ("the master gland"), and hormones feed back onto the brain — adrenaline is the
    felt ingredient of fear. The clearest architecture is negative feedback: when blood glucose
    rises, insulin lowers it; when it falls, glucagon raises it. The set-point holds because each
    deviation automatically calls up its own correction.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the broadcast network" widget="judge" config={nbHormoneJudge} />
  <p>
    A worked example shows the speed difference in practice. You step off a curb and see a bus:
    the visual signal, the decision, and the leg muscles firing are all nerve traffic — total
    latency a few hundred milliseconds. The racing heart and dry mouth you feel seconds later are
    adrenaline, released from the adrenal glands and arriving by blood flow. The nervous system
    handled the crisis before the hormonal echo of it arrived; the hormones then keep the body
    mobilised for the next hour. Fast point-to-point signalling for commands, slow broadcast
    chemistry for context — every animal with both uses the pair exactly this way.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the body runs two coordination networks — nerves for fast,
    point-to-point commands, hormones for slow, widespread, lasting broadcasts — and the bus
    at the curb shows both firing in sequence. What holds the internal world steady while
    the external one swings is the loop described next.
  </p>

  <h2>Homeostasis: holding the internal line</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Homeostasis</strong> is the maintenance of a constant internal environment, and it is
    non-negotiable: enzymes (lesson 1) work within narrow pH and temperature bands, and cells
    die outside them. The canonical control loop has three parts — <strong>receptors</strong>
    that detect a deviation, a <strong>control centre</strong> that compares it to the set point,
    and <strong>effectors</strong> that correct it. Temperature regulation shows the full
    repertoire. Too hot: vasodilation flushes blood to the skin, sweat evaporates (evaporation
    carries away about 2.4 kJ per gram), and behaviour (removing clothing) joins in. Too cold:
    vasoconstriction, shivering (muscle contraction generating heat), hair erection. Blood
    glucose regulation runs the insulin/glucagon pair; water balance runs through ADH acting on
    the kidney (the excretion lesson follows a water molecule through it). <strong>Positive
    feedback</strong> is rarer and reserved for one-shot processes — childbirth, where pressure
    triggers oxytocin, which strengthens contractions, which increases pressure, until delivery
    breaks the loop. The general lesson: negative feedback maintains, positive feedback drives
    to completion.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the control loop" widget="cloze" config={nbLoopCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "homeostasis keeps everything perfectly constant." It holds
    values within a <em>narrow range</em> around the set point, not at a fixed number. Blood
    glucose oscillates between about 4 and 6 mmol/L even in a healthy person through a day, and
    core temperature itself varies by roughly half a degree with circadian rhythm. Homeostasis is
    a dynamic balance — a thermostat with fluctuations, not a frozen number.</p>

  <h2>Plant tropism: coordination without neurons</h2>
  <p>
    Plants coordinate too, but with growth rather than movement and hormones rather than nerves.
    <strong>Tropisms</strong> are directional growth responses: shoots grow toward light
    (<strong>phototropism</strong>, driven by auxin migrating to the shaded side), roots grow
    downward (<strong>gravitropism</strong>, auxin inhibiting elongation on the lower side), and
    tendrils coil around supports (<strong>thigmotropism</strong>, touch-guided). The full
    mechanism lives in the plant biology lesson; the neurobiological point is the comparison.
    Both systems detect a stimulus, transmit a signal, and produce a response — but where a
    reflex arc finishes in 20 milliseconds, a tropism takes hours, and where animals flee, plants
    grow. Two solutions to the same physics problem of staying alive in a directional world.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="two solutions, one physics problem — drag across kingdoms" widget="compare" config={nbTropismCompare} />

  <h2>Scaling up to brains</h2>
  <p>
    A human brain carries roughly <strong>86 billion neurons and around 100 trillion
    synapses</strong> — wired not randomly but by genetic and activity-driven rules. Learning is
    rewiring: synapses strengthen or weaken with use — "neurons that fire together wire together"
    (Hebb, 1949) — the physical basis of memory and skill. Specialisation is real (visual cortex
    for sight, hippocampus for memory, cerebellum for movement timing) but no region works alone,
    and consciousness itself (still debated) presumably emerges from the integration. Evolution
    built the brain in layers: brainstem (breathing, reflexes — ancient and shared with reptiles)
    under limbic system (emotion, memory) under cortex (planning, language). The layers mostly
    cooperate; addiction and anxiety are what it looks like when older layers outvote newer ones.
    And the organ is expensive: 2% of body mass, about 20% of the resting energy budget. Thinking
    is costly — which is why brains evolved only where the payoff beat the bill.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the magnitude — the brain's power draw" widget="estimate" config={nbWattsEstimate} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>List the five parts of a reflex arc in order, and give the two reasons it is so fast.</li>
    <li>A poison blocks vesicle fusion at the synapse. What exactly stops happening?</li>
    <li>You step back off a curb; a minute later your heart is still pounding. Which system handled each phase, and why the difference?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Receptor, sensory neuron, relay neuron in the spinal cord,
    motor neuron, effector — fast because it has few synapses and skips the brain entirely;
    the brain is informed roughly 0.2–0.5 seconds later. (2) Neurotransmitter is never
    released into the 20-nanometre gap, so no signal crosses to the next cell — botulinum
    toxin acts exactly here. (3) The nervous system ran the crisis — seeing, deciding,
    jumping back within a few hundred milliseconds — while the pounding is adrenaline
    arriving by blood, the slower broadcast that keeps the body mobilised. Fast commands,
    slow context.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Behaviour is electrochemical: voltage spikes along neurons, chemistry across synapses.</li>
    <li>The reflex arc runs receptor to effector through the spinal cord — fast, protective, unlearned, finished before the brain knows.</li>
    <li>Synapses are one-way chemical relays and the body's drug interface; glutamate excites, GABA inhibits, acetylcholine fires muscles.</li>
    <li>A sense is a transducer: some form of energy converted into spikes the brain can count.</li>
    <li>Nerves are fast, specific and brief; hormones are slower, widespread and lasting — animals need both.</li>
    <li>Homeostasis is negative feedback holding a narrow range, not a frozen number; positive feedback drives one-shot events to completion.</li>
    <li>Learning is synaptic rewiring — neurons that fire together wire together.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Two sibling intermediate lessons pick up threads planted here: <strong>plant biology</strong>
    follows auxin through the tropisms this lesson compared with reflexes, and
    <strong>excretion in humans</strong> follows ADH and the kidney loop that holds water
    balance. The hormonal story returns in <strong>reproduction in humans</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The synapse is richer than the textbook sketch. A single cortical neuron receives input from
    roughly 7,000 other neurons; the "decision" at its axon hillock is a continuous integration
    of hundreds of graded potentials per second, weighted by synapse strength and position.
    Neurotransmission also comes in modes beyond the classical point-to-point: <strong>volume
    transmission</strong> spills transmitter broadly through extracellular fluid,
    <strong>neuromodulators</strong> (dopamine, serotonin) tune the gain of whole circuits for
    minutes to hours rather than triggering spikes, and <strong>glial cells</strong> — long
    dismissed as packing material — actively regulate synaptic chemistry, prune synapses during
    development, and even influence signalling. Roughly as many cells in your brain are glia as
    neurons.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plasticity has a developmental timetable. The critical period for binocular vision closes in
    early childhood — untreated squints or congenital cataracts leave permanent deficits if not
    corrected in time — while adult brains remain plastic in narrower windows (hippocampal
    neurogenesis continues lifelong, and London taxi drivers famously showed enlarged posterior
    hippocampi after years of map learning). Quantitatively, the brain's budget is striking: at
    roughly 20 watts — a dim light bulb — it computes with an efficiency that modern
    supercomputers running brain simulations cannot approach. Open questions are correspondingly
    grand: how 100 trillion synapses store a memory engram, how spikes encode meaning
    (rate? timing? correlates still debated), and whether consciousness is computable at all.
    The reflex arc you met at the start of this lesson is where the science began; the
    consciousness debate is where it still stands.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Hebb (1949); Azevedo et al., J. Comp. Neurol. (2009) on neuron counts; Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus, unit 14. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
